PRIVATE REFLECTION: { 
No previous reflection. Main valid second-exon candidates are E, D, and K. E (`GCATCTGAAACCGGTCCTG(TGG)`) and K (`CTGCTCTACCCAATCGCCA(TGG)`) both appear in the second exon with canonical TGG PAMs. D (`CAGGACCGGTTTCAGATGCG(CGG)`) also maps to the second exon on the reverse strand: its reverse complement corresponds to the plus-strand exon sequence `CGCATCTGAAACCGGTCCTG`, and its CGG PAM is canonical. 

Key disagreement: most colleagues favor E, citing exon location, valid PAM, moderate GC, no obvious low-complexity, and a U6-friendly 5′ G. A minority favor D because it is a full 20-nt protospacer and its reverse complement matches the exon. I noticed E and K may be only 19 nt if counted exactly, while D is 20 nt; the true 20-nt plus-strand target at the E/D locus is `CGCATCTGAAACCGGTCCTG(TGG)`, not E. Thus D is technically stronger if a standard 20-nt SpCas9 spacer is required. E remains plausible if 19-nt guides are acceptable. Off-target comparison between D and E/K is not fully resolved. Other options are mostly outside the second exon, invalid PAMs, too short, or repetitive (e.g., J in intron). Current lean: D, but E is the majority and may be intended if length is not strictly enforced. 
}

